        <mxCell id="d20bjUF1XWa1bvxXcyLc-48" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;rounded=0;" value="Some notes about multi-part upload:&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;1. It&#39;s &quot;multi-part&quot; upload, not &quot;multi-partite&quot; upload&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;2. For a long time, I used to think that I need to maintain chunk&#39;s metdata in DB myself. With AWS S3 multi-part upload, no need of manual chunking strategy or management:&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;UPLOAD:&lt;/div&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="30" width="336" x="-924" y="2771" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="d20bjUF1XWa1bvxXcyLc-49" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;rounded=0;" value="You register a file with AWS and upload its pieces in parallel. Once all pieces arrive, AWS automatically stitches them into a single object&amp;nbsp;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="30" width="366" x="-931" y="2899" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="d20bjUF1XWa1bvxXcyLc-50" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;rounded=0;" value="DOWNLOAD:" vertex="1">
